  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nConditions are about as pleasant and benign as you can get\ntoday. High pressure remains in control of our weather from the\nnortheast initially then a new system from the northwest through\nthe latter part of the week.\n\nA tropical system brewing in the southwestern Gulf is showing\nincreasing signs of organization this afternoon and may become a\ntropical depression later today or early tomorrow. Guidance is\nconsistent on bringing versions of this system at varying\nintensities into the north-central Gulf coast by Saturday or\nSunday for now. A shortwave trough pushing this system starts to\nprovide increased large scale ascent with associated moisture\nadvection by Saturday afternoon and evening and, as anticipated,\nrain chances are increasing Saturday PM and especially Sunday.\nPWAT values are around 2.00 inches as modeled currently and\nbouts of locally heavy rainfall appear possible. GFS tracks this\nsystem to the east of the area where the latest ECMWF tends to\nbring the remnants more directly overhead. This will depend\nlargely on when, where, and how intensely the storm develops in\nit's approach to the Gulf coast. A NOAA synoptic mission is\nunderway today to evaluate the airmass in and around the\ndeveloping storm and subsequent runs of guidance should have\nthis data factored in. With the synoptic pattern favorable for a\nprecursor rainfall event, a direct track of the actual cyclone\nremnants on top of that could lead to some flooding issues by\nSunday evening, but we will have to see how the details play\nout.\n\nBoth GFS/ECMWF clear the upper trough by Monday putting us back\nin a stable high-pressure regime with seasonal to slightly above\nnormal temperatures.",
